<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
    macOS hardened-runtime entitlements - §40.12 Step 19.
    Keep this file as narrow as possible; each entitlement widens the
    attack surface. Notarization will reject anything unused.

    - com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit: TRUE, and the sentence it replaced
      ("false. We don't JIT any code") shipped a macOS build that cannot
      start at all (row 143). Electron IS V8, and V8 JITs: under
      the hardened runtime without this entitlement it cannot reserve
      executable memory, and the process dies before any of our code runs
      with "Fatal process out of memory: Failed to reserve virtual memory
      for CodeRange". Measured on the PUBLISHED v0.338.0 artifact, not
      inferred: the app out of the published .dmg dies instantly, and the
      identical bundle re-signed with only this entitlement flipped boots,
      logs "Checking for update" and opens its websockets. Nothing caught
      it because every test runs the app UNSIGNED from the source tree,
      where the hardened runtime does not apply.
    - com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory: false, and
      it stays false. It is the wider entitlement of the pair and the
      launch above was proven with allow-jit alone.
    - com.apple.security.device.usb: required for WebHID on macOS -
      @ledgerhq/hw-transport-webhid opens the Ledger HID interface via
      IOKit, which the hardened runtime gates behind this entitlement.
      Same mechanism Trezor Bridge uses when its own HID handoff fires.
    - com.apple.security.network.client: required for xchain-sdk calls
      (REST/JSON-RPC to xchain-explorer / xchain-encoder) + electron-updater
      update manifest fetches + Trezor Connect iframe loading
      connect.trezor.io.
    - com.apple.security.files.user-selected.read-write: NOT currently
      needed - we use app.getPath('userData') for all storage. If we
      add an "export PSBT" feature that writes to a user-chosen path,
      revisit.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
    <false/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.device.usb</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
